{
  "chapter": "interface/ai-sdk",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "The AI SDK is TypeScript-only, which is why most production systems split TypeScript for",
    "the interface and Python for retrieval and evaluation.",
    "A tool-augmented chat needs an explicit stop condition or it loops, and the SDK requires",
    "one. Part II's argument is now an API parameter.",
    "Only two first-party stop conditions ship: step count and a terminal tool call. Cost and",
    "deadline remain yours, and a step cap does not notice an expensive step or a passing",
    "deadline.",
    "ToolLoopAgent is an agent loop, and so are LangGraph and a Temporal workflow. Adopting all",
    "three gives you step counters that disagree.",
    "One loop, and it lives wherever durability lives. With a durable backend that means",
    "neither framework's loop.",
    "Suggested bounds map onto this book's versions: one for one-tool-then-answer, five for",
    "chat, ten to twenty for autonomous.",
    "The boundary in one sentence: the AI SDK carries bytes to the browser; the workflow",
    "decides what happens."
  ],
  "policy": {
    "firstPartyStopConditions": [
      "step-count",
      "terminal-tool"
    ],
    "boundsYouOwn": [
      "cost",
      "deadline"
    ],
    "shapes": [
      {
        "name": "one-tool-then-answer",
        "suggestedMaxSteps": 1
      },
      {
        "name": "chat",
        "suggestedMaxSteps": 5
      },
      {
        "name": "autonomous",
        "suggestedMaxSteps": 20
      }
    ]
  },
